+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edit Policy
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers submit edits to a plot visit from tablets that may be offline for up to 48 hours. Two submissions can start from the same server state and arrive at the record store in either order, sometimes days apart. Workers also correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. The record store must decide what the current visit is when such submissions arrive, and what happens to the submission that does not win.
+
+The stakes are scientific. A visit holds observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes, and the partner's reviewed CSV is built from these values. Some edits are unrelated (correcting an observer name, adding a missing species). Others are related (revising a species identification and its cover estimate). The server cannot tell these apart from field names. An automatic combination that keeps two individually valid edits can produce a visit no worker submitted and no coordinator reviewed.
+
+This decision fixes the concurrency policy for the record-store node. Tablet-sync and review-export both size their designs around it and link back here.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Inherited gate: originals are preserved.** Every accepted submission is retained with its author, receipt time, and starting revision, under either policy. No option that discards an original is considered.
+
+**Major: a submitted visit stays intact as a worker's observation.** Tenet: *Do not silently discard a submitted edit.* An option that keeps each submission as a complete unit lets a coordinator see exactly what a worker observed.
+
+**Major: no unreviewed combination of related scientific values.** Tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable.* An option that combines edits automatically must either avoid combining related fields or accept that some combinations escape review.
+
+**Moderate: coordinator review volume.** Tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator.* Each queued conflict is coordinator work. The number of conflicts per season is not yet measured.
+
+**Moderate: maintained merge rules.** An option that needs field-specific rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries carries an ongoing maintenance cost, and the rules must be kept current as the field protocol changes.
+
+**Moderate: submission and local storage size.** Tablets hold drafts and retry state. Some options send complete visits per edit and others send only changed fields.
+
+**Minor: export readiness.** Visits excluded from the Friday export because of unresolved work reduce what the partner receives on time.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each edit names the revision it started from. An edit that starts from the current revision becomes the new current revision. An edit that starts from an older revision while a newer revision exists is stored as a competing complete revision. The visit is then in unresolved review state until a coordinator selects a revision, or creates a new one that resolves the competition. The coordinator's resolution references the competing submissions. Original submissions remain unchanged.
+
+Demonstration: a visit at revision R3 receives edit A from tablet 4 starting at R2 and edit B from tablet 9 starting at R3. A is stored as a competing revision. B becomes R4. The visit is in review until a coordinator resolves it.
+
+Analysis:
+- Preserves a submitted visit as a unit: **strong**. Each revision is exactly one worker's complete observation.
+- Avoids unreviewed combination of related values: **strong**. Nothing is combined by the server.
+- Coordinator review volume: **weak**. Concurrent edits to unrelated fields also enter review, so unrelated corrections queue together.
+- Maintained merge rules: **strong**. The policy needs no field-level rules.
+- Submission and storage size: **partial**. Each edit carries a complete visit, and the cost is proportional to visit size, which is small compared with photographs.
+- Coordinator operability: **partial**. Review work is more frequent but each item is concrete and self-contained.
+- Export readiness: **weak**. Any unresolved visit is held out of the export set.
+
+Summary: trades review workload for intact submissions and no silent combination.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each edit supplies its starting state and the fields it changed. The server applies disjoint field changes to the current state automatically. Overlapping changes to the same field, or changes that the field rules mark as dependent, are stored for review. The combined record is a new revision that no single worker submitted. Field-level provenance records which submission supplied each value.
+
+Analysis:
+- Preserves a submitted visit as a unit: **partial**. Combined records are not any worker's submission. Provenance preserves the source of each value, but the unit is lost.
+- Avoids unreviewed combination of related values: **weak**. Disjoint edits to fields that carry a scientific relationship combine automatically unless the rules anticipate that relationship, and the rules cannot anticipate all of them.
+- Coordinator review volume: **strong**. Only overlapping or dependent changes queue.
+- Maintained merge rules: **weak**. Rules are required for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and they must follow the field protocol as it changes.
+- Submission and storage size: **strong**. Submissions send changed fields only.
+- Coordinator operability: **weak**. Coordinators must understand which automatic combinations occurred to judge a combined record.
+- Export readiness: **strong**. More visits reach the export without review.
+
+Summary: trades review workload and submission size for automatic combination and a maintained rule set.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions is selected.
+
+It wins on the two major criteria. Those criteria come from the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*. Field-aware merge would give the partner more visits on time, but it would do so by combining values that no worker submitted and that the rules may not understand. For this project, a late visit is a smaller cost than a combined value nobody reviewed.
+
+The decision takes a real cost against the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. Coordinators will resolve more conflicts, including conflicts between unrelated corrections. This cost is accepted, not dismissed.
+
+Forecloses:
+- Automatic combination of concurrent edits by the record store. Each visit's current state is always a single submitted revision or a coordinator-created resolution.
+- Field-level merge rules as a server responsibility.
+
+Obligations downstream:
+- **Submission envelope.** Each submission carries the starting revision ID and an idempotency key (REC-002, REC-003).
+- **Current-state response.** A retrieval returns the current revision and whether unresolved competing revisions exist (REC-005). The two are distinct, so a usable state is never mistaken for pending review work.
+- **Unresolved-work representation.** Each competing revision is listed with its author, receipt time, and the revision it started from. The coordinator can compare any two revisions.
+- **Resolution operation.** A resolution names the chosen revision or supplies a new one, and records the coordinator's identity and the contributing submission IDs (REC-006).
+- **Tablet-sync.** Stores the base revision ID for each local draft and displays pending competing revisions to the worker. A draft started from a revision that has since been superseded must be rebased by re-submission from the current revision. Tablet-sync sizes its retry and rebase states to this.
+- **Review-export.** Provides a revision comparison view. Export eligibility excludes any visit with unresolved competing revisions. How the Friday file reports excluded visits is not decided here.
+
+### What would reopen this
+
+Reconsider field-aware merge if measured data shows one of these:
+- Concurrent conflicts routinely exceed what the two coordinators can resolve before the Friday export.
+- Most conflicts involve fields with no scientific dependency, such as observer names, and a narrow rule set covering only those fields would remove most of the queue.
+- Field-level provenance and a maintained rule set are demonstrably affordable for the team.
+
+A narrowed hybrid (automatic combination only for fields with no scientific dependency) is the most likely form of a future revision. It would be a new decision, recorded here as a replacement, not an amendment to this one.
